WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.265
Lesson 1.7.

00:00:02.265 --> 00:00:05.440
Reading signatures and simulating transactions.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.418
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:07.418 --> 00:00:09.610
able to know exactly what a signature or

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:11.855
transaction will do before you approve it.

00:00:13.155 --> 00:00:14.445
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.445 --> 00:00:16.130
First, the key ideas.

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:18.845
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:18.845 --> 00:00:20.370
Then, your checklist.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:22.532
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:23.832 --> 00:00:29.196
First, Transaction simulation: good wallets (and tools like

00:00:29.196 --> 00:00:35.007
transaction simulators) show the expected balance changes before

00:00:35.007 --> 00:00:38.347
you sign: "−100 USDC, +0.033 ETH".

00:00:38.347 --> 00:00:40.302
If the preview shows assets leaving

00:00:40.302 --> 00:00:42.177
that you didn't intend, stop.

00:00:42.177 --> 00:00:47.237
Next, Typed-data signatures (EIP-712): structured messages (orders,

00:00:47.237 --> 00:00:50.662
Permits) that your wallet shows as fields.

00:00:50.662 --> 00:00:53.767
Read the spender, token, amount and deadline.

00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:56.543
A "Permit" or "Permit2" signature can hand

00:00:56.543 --> 00:00:59.038
over token access without any gas.

00:00:59.038 --> 00:01:02.180
Then, Blind signing: when a hardware wallet can't

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:05.183
decode a transaction, it shows only a hash.

00:01:05.183 --> 00:01:08.106
Keep blind signing off by default; turn it on

00:01:08.106 --> 00:01:11.773
only for a specific, verified action, then off again.

00:01:11.773 --> 00:01:17.381
Also, Account delegation (EIP-7702): since Ethereum's 2025 Pectra

00:01:17.381 --> 00:01:21.714
upgrade, a normal wallet can sign an authorisation

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:26.178
that delegates its behaviour to a smart contract.

00:01:26.178 --> 00:01:28.730
A malicious delegation can hand control of

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:30.988
the whole account to an attacker.

00:01:30.988 --> 00:01:33.686
Only sign delegations from wallets and apps

00:01:33.686 --> 00:01:35.948
you trust, for a stated purpose.

00:01:35.948 --> 00:01:38.495
And finally, Rule: if you can't say in one

00:01:38.495 --> 00:01:41.043
sentence what a prompt does, don't sign it.

00:01:42.343 --> 00:01:44.858
Let's work an example,

00:01:44.858 --> 00:01:47.533
step by step. A site

00:01:47.533 --> 00:01:50.946
asks you to "verify your wallet". The

00:01:50.946 --> 00:01:54.898
wallet shows a typed-data message: Permit2 ·

00:01:55.598 --> 00:01:58.110
token: USDC · spender: 0x9f…c3 ·

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:01.308
amount: 115792089… (unlimited) · deadline:

00:02:02.008 --> 00:02:04.823
2030. That isn't verification: it's

00:02:04.823 --> 00:02:08.058
unlimited USDC access for an unknown

00:02:08.058 --> 00:02:10.893
spender. Reject it, close the

00:02:10.893 --> 00:02:13.683
tab, and check your approvals.

00:02:14.983 --> 00:02:16.443
Here's your checklist.

00:02:16.443 --> 00:02:17.978
Do it now, for real.

00:02:17.978 --> 00:02:22.143
First, My wallet shows simulated balance changes before signing.

00:02:22.143 --> 00:02:24.659
Next, I read spender, token, amount

00:02:24.659 --> 00:02:27.755
and deadline on every typed-data prompt.

00:02:27.755 --> 00:02:30.997
Then, Blind signing is off on my hardware wallet.

00:02:30.997 --> 00:02:32.591
And finally, I never sign

00:02:32.591 --> 00:02:35.472
account-delegation requests from unknown sites.

00:02:36.772 --> 00:02:37.982
Question 1.

00:02:37.982 --> 00:02:40.077
What does transaction simulation show?

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:47.942
The answer: The expected balance changes before you sign.

00:02:49.242 --> 00:02:50.487
Question 2.

00:02:50.487 --> 00:02:52.356
Why is a Permit signature risky

00:02:52.356 --> 00:02:53.582
if you don't read it?

00:02:58.002 --> 00:03:00.406
The answer: It can grant token access

00:03:00.406 --> 00:03:02.810
without a transaction or gas, so it's

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.582
easy to approve by accident.

00:03:05.882 --> 00:03:07.152
Question 3.

00:03:07.152 --> 00:03:08.817
When should blind signing be on?

00:03:13.237 --> 00:03:15.237
The answer: Only briefly, for a

00:03:15.237 --> 00:03:17.987
specific verified action, then switched off.

00:03:19.287 --> 00:03:20.532
Let's recap.

00:03:20.532 --> 00:03:25.540
Transaction simulation: good wallets (and tools like transaction simulators)

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:29.092
show the expected balance changes before you sign.

00:03:29.092 --> 00:03:34.232
Typed-data signatures (EIP-712): structured messages (orders.

00:03:34.232 --> 00:03:37.396
Blind signing: when a hardware wallet can't decode

00:03:37.396 --> 00:03:39.929
a transaction, it shows only a hash.

00:03:39.929 --> 00:03:47.334
Account delegation (EIP-7702): since Ethereum's 2025 Pectra upgrade.

00:03:48.634 --> 00:03:51.149
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:51.149 --> 00:03:55.319
Next up, Lesson 1.8: Privacy and physical security.
